1
00:00:02,500 --> 00:00:07,020
Right now, a machine built in 1977 is still

2
00:00:07,020 --> 00:00:10,300
sending us messages from more than twenty-four

3
00:00:10,300 --> 00:00:12,360
billion kilometres away.

4
00:00:13,617 --> 00:00:16,627
And on Mars, another machine has said nothing

5
00:00:16,627 --> 00:00:18,327
at all for eight years.

6
00:00:19,832 --> 00:00:22,262
Same agency. Same promise.

7
00:00:22,262 --> 00:00:23,932
Two very different endings.

8
00:00:25,444 --> 00:00:27,544
We're a team from Bangladesh.

9
00:00:28,801 --> 00:00:31,031
We're a developer, a researcher,

10
00:00:31,031 --> 00:00:33,151
a designer and a storyteller.

11
00:00:34,648 --> 00:00:36,928
We kept asking one question:

12
00:00:36,928 --> 00:00:38,508
when a mission goes quiet,

13
00:00:38,508 --> 00:00:40,218
where does its story go?

14
00:00:41,732 --> 00:00:44,692
Our answer is a website called Abandoned,

15
00:00:44,692 --> 00:00:46,142
But Not Forgotten.

16
00:00:46,798 --> 00:00:50,958
Since the 1960s, NASA has left hardware on

17
00:00:50,958 --> 00:00:54,158
the Moon, on Mars, and in deep space.

18
00:00:55,165 --> 00:00:57,995
The facts exist. But they're scattered across

19
00:00:57,995 --> 00:01:00,225
mission pages, press releases,

20
00:01:00,225 --> 00:01:01,525
and PDFs.

21
00:01:02,649 --> 00:01:04,739
And one word hides the truth:

22
00:01:04,739 --> 00:01:05,789
abandoned.

23
00:01:06,842 --> 00:01:09,582
Nobody drove these machines home.

24
00:01:09,582 --> 00:01:11,942
They worked until something stopped them.

25
00:01:12,612 --> 00:01:14,752
Dust. Cold. Fading power.

26
00:01:14,752 --> 00:01:15,752
Distance.

27
00:01:16,755 --> 00:01:19,625
Opportunity was built to last ninety Martian

28
00:01:19,625 --> 00:01:23,135
days. It worked for more than fourteen years.

29
00:01:24,055 --> 00:01:28,115
Then, in 2018, a global dust storm blocked

30
00:01:28,115 --> 00:01:29,205
its sunlight.

31
00:01:29,968 --> 00:01:32,668
Engineers sent more than a thousand recovery

32
00:01:32,668 --> 00:01:35,268
commands. Nothing came back.

33
00:01:36,187 --> 00:01:38,387
That's the part that matters most:

34
00:01:38,387 --> 00:01:40,037
why a mission ended.

35
00:01:40,037 --> 00:01:42,437
And it's the part hardest to find.

36
00:01:43,626 --> 00:01:46,686
A student shouldn't need ten tabs to understand it.

37
00:01:47,551 --> 00:01:49,681
So we built one place for it.

38
00:01:49,681 --> 00:01:53,461
Fifteen missions, from 1966 to today,

39
00:01:53,461 --> 00:01:57,318
across the Moon, Mars and deep space.

40
00:01:57,318 --> 00:01:59,098
Scroll the homepage,

41
00:01:59,098 --> 00:02:01,968
and the story unfolds scene by scene,

42
00:02:01,968 --> 00:02:05,143
from leaving Earth to the final silence.

43
00:02:05,143 --> 00:02:08,033
Follow six decades on the timeline,

44
00:02:08,033 --> 00:02:11,447
or find every machine on the hardware map.

45
00:02:11,447 --> 00:02:14,277
Our Final Signal page separates four moments

46
00:02:14,277 --> 00:02:16,947
most people mix up: launch,

47
00:02:16,947 --> 00:02:19,077
arrival, last contact,

48
00:02:19,077 --> 00:02:20,757
and official closure.

49
00:02:20,757 --> 00:02:24,764
Because lost and finished are not the same thing.

50
00:02:24,764 --> 00:02:28,664
Pick a spacecraft and turn it around in 3D.

51
00:02:28,664 --> 00:02:30,874
Or play Mission Detective.

52
00:02:30,874 --> 00:02:32,824
Read the clues about Spirit,

53
00:02:32,824 --> 00:02:35,214
Opportunity, or Surveyor 3,

54
00:02:35,214 --> 00:02:37,385
and work out what happened.

55
00:02:37,385 --> 00:02:39,675
There's a quiz, a mission comparison,

56
00:02:39,675 --> 00:02:42,154
and a 3D game too.

57
00:02:42,154 --> 00:02:46,385
Every fact comes from NASA and JPL sources.

58
00:02:46,385 --> 00:02:49,505
And when a machine speaks in first person,

59
00:02:49,505 --> 00:02:52,995
we label it clearly as creative interpretation,

60
00:02:52,995 --> 00:02:55,825
never as a real transmission.

61
00:02:55,825 --> 00:02:57,435
Why does this matter?

62
00:02:58,061 --> 00:03:00,861
For a student, it turns space history from

63
00:03:00,861 --> 00:03:03,191
a list of dates into a set of stories with

64
00:03:03,191 --> 00:03:06,062
an ending, and a reason.

65
00:03:06,062 --> 00:03:08,562
It's built with plain HTML,

66
00:03:08,562 --> 00:03:11,402
CSS, and JavaScript.

67
00:03:11,402 --> 00:03:13,252
No framework, no backend,

68
00:03:13,252 --> 00:03:15,536
and most of it works offline.

69
00:03:15,536 --> 00:03:17,746
So a classroom with a weak connection can

70
00:03:17,746 --> 00:03:21,036
still open it. That matters where we live.

71
00:03:21,645 --> 00:03:25,005
And it's honest. Sources are on the site,

72
00:03:25,005 --> 00:03:27,525
and the line between fact and imagination

73
00:03:27,525 --> 00:03:29,433
is never blurred.

74
00:03:29,433 --> 00:03:30,823
This is a first version,

75
00:03:30,823 --> 00:03:32,613
with fifteen missions.

76
00:03:32,613 --> 00:03:35,572
To take it further, we need three things:

77
00:03:35,572 --> 00:03:38,795
experts to review and expand the archive,

78
00:03:38,795 --> 00:03:43,364
access to more NASA imagery and 3D surface data,

79
00:03:43,364 --> 00:03:44,504
and a Bangla version,

80
00:03:44,504 --> 00:03:46,354
with teacher resources,

81
00:03:46,354 --> 00:03:48,344
so schools here can use it.

82
00:03:50,205 --> 00:03:52,255
Voyager is still talking.

83
00:03:52,255 --> 00:03:54,422
Opportunity went quiet.

84
00:03:54,422 --> 00:03:57,604
Our job is to make sure both are heard.

85
00:03:57,604 --> 00:03:59,614
Abandoned, But Not Forgotten.
